<h1>Lithium-ion technology</h1>
<p>From cathode chemistry to pack design: the components, the control electronics and the thermal behaviour that decide performance and safety.</p>
<h2>How to use this cluster</h2>
<p>Read the cluster in the order a pack is designed: chemistry first, then cell format, then thermal behaviour, then the BMS. Changing chemistry after the pack architecture is fixed is expensive, and changing the BMS after the enclosure is fixed is worse. The articles below follow that sequence and cross-link when a decision in one layer constrains another.</p>
<h2>The four decisions in a lithium pack</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chemistry.</strong> LFP favours cycle life and safety; NMC favours energy density; LTO favours power and low-temperature behaviour at a cost in energy. The chemistry sets the voltage window and the thermal risk.</li>
<li><strong>Cell format.</strong> Prismatic cells simplify packing; cylindrical cells are mature and cost-effective; pouch cells save space but need mechanical support. The format drives the assembly process and the failure mode.</li>
<li><strong>Thermal design.</strong> Air cooling is adequate at low C-rates; liquid cooling is needed for fast charge and high ambient temperature. The thermal path decides how long the pack lasts, not just how safe it is.</li>
<li><strong>BMS.</strong> Passive balancing is sufficient for well-matched cells; active balancing helps large or second-life packs. The BMS must speak the protocol of the charger and inverter around it.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What this cluster does not cover</h2>
<p>Certification, transport rules and application sizing are separate clusters. Use the safety and transport pillar for UN38.3 and modal rules, the applications hub for duty-cycle fit, and the testing site for the standards behind a performance claim.</p>

